body: "## The population\n\n`grep -rn \"Run pm init first\" src/ --include=*.ts | grep -v test` returns **48** sites. Each one repeats the same three steps:\n\n```ts\nconst pmRoot = resolvePmRoot(process.cwd(), global.path);\nif (!(await pathExists(getSettingsPath(pmRoot)))) {\n  throw new PmCliError(`Tracker is not initialized at ${pmRoot}. Run pm init first.`, EXIT_CODE.NOT_FOUND);\n}\n```\n\n## Why it matters beyond tidiness\n\nThe refusal is not merely duplicated, it is **imprecise in a way that cannot be fixed cheaply**. Three distinct states collapse into one message:\n\n- the root does not exist\n- the root exists but is a regular file\n- the root is a directory with no settings\n\nOnly the third is repairable by `pm init`, yet all three recommend it. pm-tmhs already closed one shape of this defect (recovery re-suggested `pm init` when a `--pm-path` tracker existed). The class recurred in a new shape - discovered via pm-6xlyss, where a root that is a regular file still recommends `pm init`. It will keep recurring while the guard has 48 owners.\n\n## Shape of the fix\n\n`core/store/item-store.ts` already contains the precise logic: `assertItemMetadataRoot` distinguishes `tracker_root_missing`, `tracker_root_not_directory`, and walks ancestors to attribute the cause. That analysis is the right one and is already tested. It is simply not what the 48 entrypoints call.\n\nPromote it to a single SDK guard, delegate all 48 sites, and declare the set in the surface-replication gate so a 49th copy fails CI rather than review."
